In article <1992Jan25.002529.14416@reed.edu> nelson@reed.edu (Nelson Minar) writes:
>Since I've been playing nethack for a month or more now, and still
>can't solve the bugger (never even gotten to Medusa!) I thought it
>best to ask for a few hints.
>
>How do you play? How often do you backtrack? I tend to go straight
>down.  Not too fast, because I don't want to die. I try to be the same
>level as the dlevel, up to about level 7, and then I just play until
>I get stuck (which is too often).

 Personally, I explore every level fully, then go down the stairs to the next
one. If you couldn't handle the heat on level x, it's unlikely you'll be able
to survive on level x+1...conversely, if you hang around too long on one level
the game just generates lots of lovely wandering monsters...which is okay
until you get a bunch of hill orcs, or lower down, some giants or whatever...
Also, different character types gain levels faster than others...

>Is it efficient to move up at times, either to rest, to pick up old
>items, to reuse an altar, or just to hang out? I'm always afraid of
>running out of food.

 If you're having the crap beaten out of you in a fight, going upstairs to
rest is a good idea if you can manage it, since it only generates monsters on
the level you are on...going back to use altars for whatever purpose is a good
idea, especially if they are of your own alignment...but as you say, if you're
sacrificing your food supply you could run into trouble, especially if you're
a wizard...

>Also, is there really a best character? Priests are nice for their
>ability to identify cursed things, Wizards seem to be the most
>powerful ultimately if they manage to survive, and Barbarians do great
>at the beginning, but seem to have a low life expectancy at dlevel >
>10.

 I think Barbarians and Valkyries are the easiest to play, but also the least
challenging...Wizards are fun because of their magical items, but how many
people _really_ use the feeble spell system that often? Priests are cool, they
can really kick butt early on, as can Samurais...at the moment I'm going
through my Indiana Jones phase, using the Archaelogist's tinning kit and
pick axe is really cool - and once you've had a few blessed gain abilities, a
couple of wishes and a bit of luck (with a blessed luckstone), you can do
really well with any type of character. I was recently killed by the ol' Wiz
on level 47 when I was a level 27 Archaelogist, and the real smart arses here
have completed the game with Healers, etc. Each type of character requires
a different style of play though.

 Hope this helped,

Huggy hopes one day to finish this damn game
